8 0 obj<</K[6 34818 0 R 34819 0 R 9 34820 0 R 11]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1469 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34669 0 obj<</K[13 34812 0 R 15 34813 0 R 17 34814 0 R 19]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1469 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34670 0 obj<</K[21 34806 0 R 23 34807 0 R 25 34808 0 R 27]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1469 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34671 0 obj<</K[<</MCID 0/Pg 1472 0 R/Type/MCR>><</MCID 1/Pg 1472 0 R/Type/MCR>><</MCID 2/Pg 1472 0 R/Type/MCR>>]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1469 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34672 0 obj<</K[4 34804 0 R 6]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1472 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34673 0 obj<</K[7 34800 0 R 9 34801 0 R 11]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1472 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34674 0 obj<</K 13/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1472 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34675 0 obj<</A 34799 0 R/Alt(Bar chart compares accuracy and timeout percentage for four methods: Pommeret, Bordas, Neor, and Neur-It. Accuracy is shown in blue bars on the left vertical axis, with Pommeret, Bordas, and Neur-It near 100%, and Neor lower around 90%. Timeout percentage\ is in orange bars on the right vertical axis, with Pommeret and Bordas around 40%, Neor near zero, and Neur-It about 10%. The chart highlights that while Pommeret and Bordas have high accuracy, they also have high timeout rates, whereas Neor has lower ac\curacy but almost no timeouts, and Neur-It balances high accuracy with low timeouts. This helps understand trade-offs between accuracy and speed for these methods.␀)/K 14/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1472 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj34676 0 obj<</K 15/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1472 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj34677 0 obj<</K[18<</MCID 0/Pg 1475 0 R/Type/MCR>><</MCID 1/Pg 1475 0 R/Type/MCR>><</MCID 2/Pg 1475 0 R/Type/MCR>>]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1472 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34678 0 obj<</K[4 34798 0 R]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1475 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34679 0 obj<</K[6 34792 0 R 8 34793 0 R 10 34794 0 R 12]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1475 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34680 0 obj<</K 14/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1475 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34681 0 obj<</K[16 34780 0 R 18 34781 0 R 20 34782 0 R 22 34783 0 R 24 34784 0 R 26 34785 0 R 28]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1475 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34682 0 obj<</K[29 34766 0 R 31 34767 0 R 33 34768 0 R 35 34769 0 R 37 34770 0 R 39 34771 0 R 41 34772 0 R 43<</MCID 0/Pg 1478 0 R/Type/MCR>><</MCID 1/Pg 1478 0 R/Type/MCR>><</MCID 2/Pg 1478 0 R/Type/MCR>>]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1475 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34683 0 obj<</A 34765 0 R/Alt(Accuracy generally improves as beam width increases, reaching near perfect accuracy at beam widths of 20 and above, though variability in accuracy also increases slightly. Accuracy rises quickly with formula length from 2 to 4, then stabilizes around 0.88\ for longer formulas. Two implementation methods, sum-based and min-max, show similar high accuracy near 90%, with min-max having slightly higher median accuracy. Runtime increases steadily with beam width, starting near zero seconds and reaching about 9 \seconds at beam width 100. Runtime also grows with formula length, rising sharply after length 4 and reaching nearly 40 seconds at length 9. Between implementations, min-max runs faster on average than sum-based, with median runtimes around 25 and 35 seco\nds respectively, though sum-based shows more variability. These charts highlight the trade-off between accuracy and runtime across beam widths, formula sizes, and implementation methods.␀)/K 5/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1478 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj34684 0 obj<</K 6/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1478 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj34685 0 obj<</K[7 34759 0 R 9 34760 0 R 11 34761 0 R 13]/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1478 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34686 0 obj<</K 15/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1478 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34687 0 obj<</K 17/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1478 0 R/S/H2/Type/StructElem>>endobj34688 0 obj<</K 19/P 34567 0 R/Pg 1478 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj34689 0 obj<</K[20<</MCID 0/Pg 1481 0 R/Type/MCR>><</MCID 1/Pg 1481 0 R/Type/MC