er Elimination through QEPcad to handle complex logical parts. The procedure defines a subprogram RProg that decides the outcome \based on conditions involving functions f sub small i of X. The main goal is to return a program that confirms whether the original logical statement holds under the given constraints. This explanation helps understand how the procedure systematically bre\aks down and solves the logical problem within a set time.␀)/K 2/P 41790 0 R/Pg 941 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj41882 0 obj<</K 3/P 41790 0 R/Pg 941 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj41883 0 obj<</A 42197 0 R/Alt(A step-by-step procedure named SevenNQA is described to check if a logical statement involving variables X and Y meets a certain specification, with a limit on the number of steps allowed. The process starts with a count at zero and repeatedly applies a f\unction called RSolve to simplify the problem until a condition is met or the timeout occurs. It uses a method called Quantifier Elimination through QEPcad to handle complex logical parts. The procedure defines a subprogram RProg that decides the outcome \based on conditions involving functions f sub small i of X. The main goal is to return a program that confirms whether the original logical statement holds under the given constraints. This explanation helps understand how the procedure systematically bre\aks down and solves the logical problem within a set time.␀)/K 4/P 41790 0 R/Pg 941 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj41884 0 obj<</P 41790 0 R/Pg 941 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj41885 0 obj<</K[6 42195 0 R 8]/P 41790 0 R/Pg 941 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj41886 0 obj<</K[9 42193 0 R 11]/P 41790 0 R/Pg 941 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj41887 0 obj<</K[12 42191 0 R 14]/P 41790 0 R/Pg 941 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj41888 0 obj<</K[15 42189 0 R 17]/P 41790 0 R/Pg 941 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj41889 0 obj<</K[18 42187 0 R 20]/P 41790 0 R/Pg 941 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj4