ms\)         and a Eunoia proof script \(.pr\f.eo\).         Right: LambdaPi artefacts---a translated CPC signature plus Prelude,         and a LambdaPi proof file \(.prf.lp\).         Horizontal arrows: cvc5 maps SMT-LIB fragments to Eunoia proof scripts;         eo2lp maps both the Eunoia signatur\e and proof script to LambdaPi.        Downward arrows: ethos checks the Eunoia bundle, lambdapi check verifies         the LambdaPi bundle, each producing a tick-or-cross result.\r\n␀)/K 2/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43009 0 obj<</K 3/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj43010 0 obj<</K[4 43634 0 R 6]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43011 0 obj<</K 9/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/H3/Type/StructElem>>endobj43012 0 obj<</K[10 43624 0 R 12 43625 0 R 14 43626 0 R 16 43627 0 R 18 43628 0 R 20]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43013 0 obj<</K[21 43598 0 R 23 43599 0 R 25 43600 0 R 27 43601 0 R 29 43602 0 R 31 43603 0 R 33 43604 0 R 35 43605 0 R 37 43606 0 R 39 43607 0 R 41 43608 0 R 43 43609 0 R 45 43610 0 R 47]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43014 0 obj<</K 50/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/H4/Type/StructElem>>endobj43015 0 obj<</K[51 43592 0 R 53 43593 0 R 55 43594 0 R 57]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43016 0 obj<</K 58/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/H2/Type/StructElem>>endobj43017 0 obj<</K 60/P 42995 0 R/Pg 771 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43018 0 obj<</A 43591 0 R/Alt(Five stacked grammar tables.         First: terms---symbols, literals, applications, or binder applications;         literals as int\(n\), rat\(n,m\), or str\(s\); parameters and program cases.         Second: parameter attributes \(:implicit, :list\) and\ constant attributes         \(right/left-associative with optional nil terminator, chainable, pairwise,         binder, arg-list\).         Third: built-in symbols grouped by family---core \(eo::eq, eo::requires,         eo::quote, eo::var\), boolean \(e\o::and, eo::or, eo::not, eo::xor\),         arithmetic \(eo::add, eo::mul, eo::neg, eo::zdiv\), and         list \(eo::nil, eo::cons, eo::list\\_concat, eo::list\\_len\).         Fourth: standard commands---declare-const, declare-parameterized-const,     \    declare-consts, define \(macro definition\), program \(with signature and         cases\), declare-rule \(with optional premises/premise-list, args,         requires, and conclusion attributes\), include.         Fifth: proof commands---assume, assume\-push, step \(with rule, optional         premises, optional args\), and step-pop.\r\n␀)/K 2/P 42995 0 R/Pg 774 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43019 0 obj<</K 3/P 42995 0 R/Pg 774 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj43020 0 obj<</K[4 43589 0 R 6]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 774 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43021 0 obj<</K 8/P 42995 0 R/Pg 774 0 R/S/H3/Type/StructElem>>endobj43022 0 obj<</K[10 43587 0 R 12]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 774 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43023 0 obj<</K[43582 0 R 43583 0 R 43584 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/S/Table/Type/StructElem>>endobj43024 0 obj<</K[4 43578 0 R 6 43579 0 R 8]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 777 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43025 0 obj<</K[9 43576 0 R 11]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 777 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43026 0 obj<</K 13/P 42995 0 R/Pg 777 0 R/S/H3/Type/StructElem>>endobj43027 0 obj<</K 15/P 42995 0 R/Pg 777 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43028 0 obj<</K[43551 0 R 43552 0 R 43553 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/S/L/Type/StructElem>>endobj43029 0 obj<</K 13/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43030 0 obj<</A 43549 0 R/Alt(Four logical rules are defined using a formal syntax. The first rule, named "modus_ponens," states that if formula F1 implies formula F2, and F1 is true, then F2 is true. The second rule, "cmt_implies_pos," states that if it is not the case that F1 implie\s F2, then either F1 is true and F2 is false, or F1 is false and F2 is true. The third rule, "reordering," states that if a list includes either C1 or C2, then C2 is true. The fourth rule, "and_intro," states that if both F and F are true, then F is true.\ These rules express basic logical inference patterns using capital letters for formulas and variables.␀)/K[14 43550 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43031 0 obj<</K 17/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43032 0 obj<</A 43547 0 R/Alt(Modus ponens for a proposition p is shown as logically equivalent to the combination of three proofs: proof F1, proof that F1 implies F2, and proof F2. This expresses that having a proof of p and a proof that p implies another statement allows deriving a \proof of that statement.␀)/K[18 43548 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43033 0 obj<</K 20/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43034 0 obj<</A 43545 0 R/Alt(The expression states that a condition named "cnf_implies_pos" applied to a variable phi implies that the function "quote" applied to the implication from F1 to F2 holds. This is followed by a reference to the proof of phi. It shows a logical relationship\ where one condition leads to another, with a proof linked to the original variable.␀)/K[21 43546 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43035 0 obj<</K 23/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43036 0 obj<</A 43543 0 R/Alt(The expression defines a function named "reordering" with input small p in parentheses. It shows that "reordering of small p" is equal to the combination of three parts: "eco quote capital C2," "Proof capital C1," and "Proof small phi superscript star." T\his suggests that the reordering function is constructed by linking these three components together, likely representing a step or result in a mathematical proof or process.␀)/K[24 43544 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43037 0 obj<</K 26/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43038 0 obj<</A 43541 0 R/Alt(The expression defines a function named "and_intro" that takes an input called "p" and returns a pair consisting of two proofs, both related to "p." This shows how to introduce a logical "and" by combining two proofs of the same statement.␀)/K[27 43542 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 780 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43039 0 obj<</K 3/P 42995 0 R/Pg 783 0 R/S/H3/Type/StructElem>>endobj43040 0 obj<</K[5 43539 0 R 7]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 783 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43041 0 obj<</K 8/P 42995 0 R/Pg 783 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43042 0 obj<</K 9/P 42995 0 R/Pg 783 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43043 0 obj<</K 10/P 42995 0 R/Pg 783 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43044 0 obj<</K[43504 0 R 43505 0 R 43506 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/S/L/Type/StructElem>>endobj43045 0 obj<</K 7/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43046 0 obj<</A 43502 0 R/Alt(Three lines of code define logical operators and a binder in a programming language. The first line declares a constant named "or" that takes two Boolean inputs and returns a Boolean, with a property indicating right associativity and no automatic simplif\ication. The second line declares a constant named "implies" that takes two inputs of any type A and returns a Boolean, marked as chainable. The third line declares a constant named "forall" that takes a function from any type A to a list of Booleans and \returns a Boolean, marked as a binder. This code sets up basic logical operations and quantification for use in formal proofs or programming.␀)/K[8 43503 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43047 0 obj<</K 12/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43048 0 obj<</A 43500 0 R/Alt(The expression defines a function named "elab" with input variables small x, small y, and small z. It states that the function returns true if and only if all three variables small x, small y, and small z are false. This is shown by the function equaling \the logical AND of the negations of small x, small y, and small z. The purpose is to express that the combined condition of small x OR small y OR small z being true is equivalent to the negation of each variable individually ANDed together, highlighting a\ logical relationship between these variables.␀)/K[13 43501 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43049 0 obj<</K 15/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43050 0 obj<</A 43498 0 R/Alt(The elaboration function applied to the sequence x comma y of the expression "a equals b c d" is equal to the elaboration function applied to the sequence x comma y of the conjunction of "a equals b," "b equals c," and "c equals d." This shows that elabor\ating a chain of equalities is equivalent to elaborating each equality step separately and combining them with logical and.␀)/K[16 43499 0 R]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43051 0 obj<</K 18/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43052 0 obj<</K 19/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43053 0 obj<</K 21/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/H2/Type/StructElem>>endobj43054 0 obj<</K[23 43496 0 R 25]/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43055 0 obj<</K 26/P 42995 0 R/Pg 786 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43056 0 obj<</A 43495 0 R/Alt(Top: term grammar for the lambda-Pi calculus modulo rewriting.         Universes mu range over TYPE and KIND.         Terms t range over variables, constants, universes, applications,         lambda abstractions, and dependent products.         Bottom: na\tural-deduction typing rules---empty context is well-formed         \(wf0\); extending with \(x:t\) requires Gamma deriving t at some universe         \(wf+\); variable rule reads \(x:t\) from the context \(var\);         constant rule reads \(kappa:t\) f\rom the signature with the constant's         type sortable in Gamma \(con\); TYPE has type KIND \(univ\);         dependent product, abstraction, application,