t of stably infinite theories with computable spectra. The set of stably infinite theories lies above the set of \stably infinite theories with computable spectra. The set of gentle theories and the set of stably infinite theories with computable spectra lies above the set of stably infinite gentle theories, which lies above the set of shiny theories.\r\n\r\n\r\n␀)/K 77/P 43700 0 R/Pg 715 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43719 0 obj<</K 78/P 43700 0 R/Pg 715 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj43720 0 obj<</K[81 45019 0 R 83 45020 0 R]/P 43700 0 R/Pg 715 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43721 0 obj<</K[1 45007 0 R 3 45008 0 R 5 45009 0 R 7 45010 0 R 9 45011 0 R 11 45012 0 R 13]/P 43700 0 R/Pg 718 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43722 0 obj<</A 45006 0 R/Alt(The expressions define a wedge product operation on vectors labeled w sub 1 through w sub n. The wedge product of these vectors equals the intersection over i from 1 to n of the vectors w sub i, which also equals the wedge product of vectors v sub 1 throu\gh v sub n. The vector v sub i is defined as the wedge product of w sub 1 through w sub i minus 1 and w sub i plus 1 through w sub n. The vector psi sub 2n is defined as the wedge product of w sub 1 through w sub n plus the wedge product of v sub 1 throug\h v sub n. Finally, psi is defined as the intersection of psi sub 2n and psi sub n. This set of formulas shows how complex vector operations can be broken down into simpler wedge products and intersections, useful in advanced geometry or algebra contexts.\␀)/K 14/P 43700 0 R/Pg 718 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj43723 0 obj<</K 15/P 43700 0 R/Pg 718 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj43724 0 obj<</K 18/P 43700 0 R/Pg 718 0 R/S/H2/Type/StructElem>>endobj43725 0 obj<</K 21/P 43700 0 R/Pg 718 0 R/S/H3/Type/StructElem>>endobj43726 0 obj<</K[23 45000 0 R 25 45001 0 R 28 45002 0 R 31 45003 0 R 34 45004 0 R 37]/P 43700 0 R/Pg 718 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj43727 0 obj<</K[38 