K 18/P 49239 0 R/Pg 432 0 R/S/H5/Type/StructElem>>endobj49279 0 obj<</K 19/P 49239 0 R/Pg 432 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49280 0 obj<</A 49444 0 R/Alt(A theorem states that for natural numbers n, m, and k, shifting n by adding k and then shifting m by n is equal to shifting m by n plus k and then shifting n. A pattern definition shows that shifting left by adding k to zero equals shifting zero by k, wit\h the condition that m is not zero. This explains a property of shifting operations in a formal system, highlighting how shifts combine and the special case when shifting zero.)/K[20 49445 0 R]/P 49239 0 R/Pg 432 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj49281 0 obj<</K 3/P 49239 0 R/Pg 435 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49282 0 obj<</K 6/P 49239 0 R/Pg 435 0 R/S/H5/Type/StructElem>>endobj49283 0 obj<</K 7/P 49239 0 R/Pg 435 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49284 0 obj<</A 49442 0 R/Alt(The statement defines a theorem about a property called "big step." It says that for any t, the property holds if and only if either there is a condition called "ifThenElse" involving B, S, T, and t, or t is related to H and G, S, and t in a specific way.\ The theorem uses logical "and" and "or" to combine these conditions and concludes with a proof reference named "grind." This expresses a precise logical relationship important for understanding how the "big step" property works in this context.)/K[8 49443 0 R]/P 49239 0 R/Pg 435 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj49285 0 obj<</K 11/P 49239 0 R/Pg 435 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49286 0 obj<</K 14/P 49239 0 R/Pg 435 0 R/S/H5/Type/StructElem>>endobj49287 0 obj<</K 15/P 49239 0 R/Pg 435 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49288 0 obj<</A 49440 0 R/Alt(Defines a mathematical structure called a vector with elements of any type, using natural numbers for indexing. It includes two cases: an empty vector labeled 'nil' and a constructor labeled 'cons' that adds an element to the vector. A function named 'hea\d' is defined to extract the first element of a non-empty vector. An example shows how to add the first elements of two vectors by matching their structure and summing their heads, illustrating the use of the 'head' function and vector construction. The c\ode demonstrates basic vector operations and pattern matching in a formal language.)/K[16 49441 0 R]/P 49239 0 R/Pg 435 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj49289 0 obj<</K 18/P 49239 0 R/Pg 435 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49290 0 obj<</K 3/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/H2/Type/StructElem>>endobj49291 0 obj<</K[5 49436 0 R 7 49437 0 R 9]/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49292 0 obj<</K 12/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/H3/Type/StructElem>>endobj49293 0 obj<</K 13/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49294 0 obj<</A 49434 0 R/Alt(An example shows a system of three equations with three unknowns labeled a, b, and c. The first equation is a plus b plus c equals 3. The second equation is a squared plus b squared plus c squared equals 5. The third equation is a cubed plus b cubed plus \c cubed equals 7. The solution to this system is given as a equals 1, b equals 2, and c equals 0, found using the method called grind. This demonstrates solving a set of nonlinear equations with three variables.)/K[14 49435 0 R]/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj49295 0 obj<</K 16/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49296 0 obj<</A 49432 0 R/Alt(An example shows a mathematical expression where the ring of polynomials with variable small x over the finite field with eight elements is considered. The expression states that the quotient of this polynomial ring by the ideal generated by the polynomia\l small x to the sixteenth power minus small x equals the product of the polynomials small x, small x plus one, small x plus capital omega, and small x plus capital omega squared. The text explains that this holds because sixteen equals sixteen times sixt\een, which equals two hundred fifty-six, or two to the eighth power. It notes that the ring solver handles commutative semiring operations but cannot perform subtraction, so it uses the Grothendieck completion to obtain a ring. This explanation highlights\ how the example demonstrates the use of algebraic structures and computational methods to work with polynomial rings over finite fields.)/K[17 49433 0 R]/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj49297 0 obj<</A 49430 0 R/Alt(An example shows a sequence of algebraic steps using variables x, y, and alpha. It starts with the expression x squared minus 2 times y equals 1, then transforms to x times y squared equals y, then to y times x equals 1, and finally to x times x equals 1.\ The process is labeled as "commuting alpha" and "add right cancel alpha," and the final step is noted as done by "grind." This illustrates a method of simplifying or manipulating algebraic expressions step-by-step.)/K[20 49431 0 R]/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj49298 0 obj<</K 25/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/H4/Type/StructElem>>endobj49299 0 obj<</K[26 49428 0 R 28]/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49300 0 obj<</A 49426 0 R/Alt(Defines an example named "a" with type "unsigned 32-bit integer." The example states that "a squared equals b cubed," and concludes that "a plus b squared equals b." The proof is indicated to be done by a method called "grind." This shows a simple mathema\tical relationship and a proof step in a programming or formal verification context.)/K[29 49427 0 R]/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj49301 0 obj<</K 31/P 49239 0 R/Pg 438 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj49302 0 obj<</A 49423 0 R/Alt(An example showing a sequence of elements labeled a, b, c, d, and a relation symbolized by a small letter alpha. The sequence illustrates that a is related to b, which is related to c, then to d, and back to a, forming a cycle. The notation highlights the\ 